+## Conversations: a new chat vs. resuming the same thread
+
+Every call opens the provider's **home URL** in a fresh tab, and for most sites
+that home URL *is* a brand-new conversation. So:
+
+- **New chat (default).** `defaults.newChat: true` also clicks the site's own
+  "New chat" control when one exists, so a previous thread cannot bleed into the
+  answer. This is what you want for one-off, self-contained subtasks.
+- **Resume a thread you care about → use `--session <name>`.** Do **not** rely on
+  `--no-new-chat`: it only skips clicking "New chat", but the tab has already been
+  navigated to the site root (a new chat), so the previous context is gone.
+  Give the conversation a name instead:
+
+  ```bash
+  # 1st call: creates the conversation, remembers its URL under "todo-html"
+  ask-web-ai ask --session todo-html --provider deepseek \
+    --file change1.txt --attach ./app.html --json
+
+  # later call: navigates straight back into that SAME conversation, so the
+  # web AI still remembers app.html — no re-upload needed
+  ask-web-ai ask --session todo-html --provider deepseek \
+    --file change2.txt --json
+  ```
+
+The name → conversation-URL map lives in `~/.agent-web-ai/chats.json`. You map a
+task to its URL once and reuse it; the site's own title is irrelevant. Manage it:
+
+```bash
+ask-web-ai chats                 # list saved conversations
+ask-web-ai chats show todo-html  # one entry (prints the URL)
+ask-web-ai chats rename old new  # rename the LOCAL name (URL unchanged)
+ask-web-ai chats rm todo-html    # forget it
+```
+
+`--chat-url <url>` resumes an explicit URL directly (implies `--no-new-chat`).
+
+You never need to ask the web AI to "start a new conversation" — that is the
+tool's job, not the prompt's.
+
+> Pitfall — entry point: run the CLI as `node bin/ask-web-ai.js ...` (or the
+> installed `ask-web-ai`). Running `src/cli.js` directly does nothing — that file
+> only exports `main()`, so the process exits 0 with no output at all.
+
+## Writing the prompt
+
+The web AI sees **only** the prompt — no conversation, no files, no repository.
+Every prompt must therefore be self-contained and shaped in five parts:
+
+1. instruction — "You are a careful editor."
+2. task verb — summarise / translate / classify / extract / rewrite
+3. input, **delimited** — `<text>…</text>`, `---` fences, or `INPUT: … END INPUT`
+4. output format, stated exactly, with a one-line example
+5. constraint — "Output only the JSON. No preamble, no explanation."
+
+The two most common failures are omitting (4) and (5): the model answers with a
+question, or wraps the result you wanted inside three paragraphs of commentary.
+Also state the **output language** explicitly (a site may default to its own UI
+language), and never paste secrets — the prompt goes to a third-party website.
+
+For long input, avoid shell escaping entirely:
+
+```bash
+ask-web-ai ask --file ./subtask.txt -p duckai --json
+cat notes.md | ask-web-ai ask --stdin -p duckai --json
+```
+
+Full guide with copy-paste templates: [`docs/prompting.md`](../../docs/prompting.md)
+(中文: [`docs/prompting.zh-CN.md`](../../docs/prompting.zh-CN.md)).
+
 ## Rules
 
 1. **One subtask per call.** Prompts must be self-contained: the web AI cannot
